  "worked_solution": "To estimate the number of students who like biology or chemistry but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\"Like chemistry\"** or the column **\" Like biology\"**, but not in both, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\n|  | Like biology | Doesn't like biology | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Like chemistry | $25$ | $19$ | $44$ |\n| Doesn't like chemistry | $8$ | $10$ | $18$ |\n| Totals | $33$ | $29$ | $62$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(62),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8+19}{62}=\\frac{27}{62}=0.435.\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student likes biology or chemistry but not both.\n\nFinally, to find the estimated number of students of Edison High School who like biology or chemistry but not both, we multiply the total number of students in the Edison High School $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =500\\cdot p \\\\  & =500\\cdot 0.435 \\\\  & =217.5 \\\\  & =218,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.",
